"""Original KS3 revision content, following the supplied teaching plans."""
K={}
def skill(key,title,idea,detail,example,task,scheme,mistake,model=None):
 K[key]=dict(key=key,title=title,notes=[idea,detail],worked=example,question=task,scheme=scheme.split('~'),mistake=mistake,model=model)

skill('safety','Laboratory safety, equipment and hazards','A hazard is something that could cause harm; risk considers how likely harm is and how serious it could be. A risk assessment links a specific hazard to a control that reduces risk.','Goggles protect eyes from splashes. A measuring cylinder measures liquid volume more precisely than an ungraduated beaker. Read the bottom of a water meniscus at eye level. Follow the teacher’s method and report broken glass or spills.','For heating water, hot glass is the hazard, burns are the possible harm, and a heatproof mat plus allowing cooling reduces the risk. “Be careful” alone is not a specific control.','Explain how to measure 25 cm³ of water and give a suitable control for a splash risk.','Use a measuring cylinder with a suitable scale~Read the meniscus at eye level~Wear eye protection to reduce the risk to eyes','Hazard and risk are related but do not mean the same thing.')
skill('variables','Testable questions, predictions and variables','A scientific question must connect to observations or measurements that could test it. The independent variable is changed, the dependent variable is measured and control variables are kept consistent.','State a prediction with a reason from scientific ideas. Define a useful range and intervals, identify measurable outcomes and control other factors that could affect them. Not every investigation is a fair test: some observe patterns or classify.','To test the effect of water temperature on dissolving time, change temperature, measure time until a fixed sugar mass dissolves, and control water volume, stirring and particle size.','Identify the independent, dependent and two control variables when testing how light intensity affects pondweed oxygen production.','Independent: light intensity or a stated proxy such as lamp distance~Dependent: oxygen volume per unit time~Control temperature and carbon dioxide availability','A prediction should be testable, rather than an unsupported guess.')
skill('planning','Plan fair tests, measurements and repeats','A method must say what to do, what to measure, the units, the range and how to reduce risks. Resolution is the smallest detectable change. Accuracy concerns closeness to the true value; precision concerns the spread of repeated values.','Choose equipment with suitable range and resolution, check zero readings, repeat each condition and calculate a mean when appropriate. Repeatability uses the same method and setup; reproducibility compares different people or equipment.','Timing ten oscillations and dividing by ten reduces the fractional effect of reaction time compared with timing just one. Repeating reduces random variation but cannot remove a miscalibrated clock.','Explain two ways to improve a measurement of the time for one pendulum swing.','Time several swings and divide by their number~Repeat and calculate a mean~Use the same start/finish position and small release angle','Repeated measurements do not fix a systematic offset.')
skill('burner','Bunsen burners and complete combustion','A Bunsen burner mixes gas with air before combustion. With the air hole closed, the yellow safety flame is visible and generally cooler. Opening the air hole admits more oxygen and produces a hotter blue heating flame.','Identify the base, barrel, collar, air hole and gas inlet. Use the school’s supervised lighting routine, tie back hair, clear the workspace and use a heatproof mat. Never leave a heating flame unattended.','A yellow flame can deposit soot because combustion is incomplete. More air supports more complete combustion; the blue flame is used for controlled heating under teacher supervision.','Explain why the air hole changes the flame and give one safety precaution when heating.','Air hole changes oxygen supply~More air supports more complete combustion and a blue flame~Tie back hair or use a heatproof mat or another specific valid control','A blue flame can be difficult to see and must still be treated as hot.')
skill('data','Tables, graphs and conclusions','A results table puts quantities and units in headings, then records measurements consistently. Continuous data can vary within a range; discrete data are counted; categories describe types.','Use a scatter graph for paired continuous quantities and a bar chart for categories. Put the independent variable on the horizontal axis. Choose a sensible scale, plot accurately and use a suitable trend line. A conclusion links the pattern to the question with numerical evidence.','For temperatures 20, 30 and 40°C with times 80, 55 and 36 s, a conclusion is that higher temperature reduced dissolving time over this range; it fell by 44 s from 20 to 40°C.','Describe a suitable graph for temperature against dissolving time and write one feature of a strong conclusion.','Scatter graph with temperature on x and time on y~Label axes with units and use a suitable trend~Support the conclusion with paired numerical evidence','Correlation alone does not establish a causal mechanism.')
skill('evaluate','Random errors, systematic errors and evaluation','Random errors make repeated readings vary unpredictably. Systematic errors shift readings in a consistent direction. An anomaly is a result that does not fit the overall pattern and should be investigated.','Evaluate whether evidence supports the claim, using range, repeats, uncertainty and controls. Repeat a suspect measurement and record reasons before excluding it. Suggest a specific improvement linked to an identified limitation.','A balance reading 2 g before an object is added produces an offset. Taring fixes that systematic error. Repeating without taring gives precise but inaccurate masses.','A thermometer always reads 2°C too high. Identify the error type and explain why averaging repeats will not correct it.','Systematic error~Every measurement has the same positive offset~Calibration/correction is needed; averaging retains the offset','Precision does not guarantee accuracy.')
skill('microscope','Observing cells and magnification','Cells are the basic structural units of organisms. A light microscope uses an objective and an eyepiece lens to magnify a specimen. Total magnification equals eyepiece magnification times objective magnification.','Start with the lowest-power objective and focus carefully, following the teacher’s instructions. Make clear pencil drawings with single outlines, proportional structures and ruled labels. Magnification and resolution are different: enlarging a blur does not reveal new detail.','A ×10 eyepiece and ×40 objective give ×400 magnification. A 20 mm drawing of a 0.05 mm cell has magnification 20/0.05=400, with lengths first in the same units.','A microscope has a ×10 eyepiece and ×20 objective. Calculate total magnification and explain why units must match in image-size calculations.','10×20=×200~Magnification is a ratio~Image and actual lengths must use the same units','Magnification has no unit of length.', 'cell')
skill('cell','Plant and animal cells','Animal and plant cells share a cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus, mitochondria and ribosomes. The membrane controls exchange; the nucleus contains genetic material. Many plant cells also have a cellulose wall and a permanent vacuole.','Chloroplasts absorb light for photosynthesis in photosynthetic plant cells. Not all plant cells contain chloroplasts, for example most root cells. Mitochondria are involved in aerobic respiration in both plants and animals.','A leaf cell has chloroplasts because it is exposed to light. A root hair cell instead has a long extension suited to absorbing water and mineral ions. Both remain plant cells with walls.','Compare a typical leaf cell with an animal cell, giving one shared structure and two differences.','Shared structure with correct function, e.g. nucleus contains genetic material~Leaf cell has a cellulose cell wall; animal cell does not~Leaf cell has chloroplasts and usually a large permanent vacuole','Plants respire as well as photosynthesise.', 'cell')
skill('specialised','Specialised and unicellular organisms','A specialised cell has features suited to a function. Structure–function explanations say how a feature helps, rather than simply naming it. A unicellular organism carries out its life processes within one cell.','A sperm cell has a tail for movement and many mitochondria to support it. A red blood cell has a biconcave shape and no nucleus in mature humans, leaving room for haemoglobin. A root hair increases absorption area.','A unicellular organism such as an amoeba takes in food, exchanges substances and responds to its surroundings. It does not need organs, because the entire organism is one cell.','Explain two adaptations of a root hair cell.','Long hair-like extension~Larger surface area for absorption~Many mitochondria support active uptake of mineral ions','A unicellular organism is a living organism, not an incomplete multicellular animal.')
skill('diffusion','Diffusion and exchange at cells','Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to lower concentration because of random motion. Individual particles continue moving in both directions, including at equilibrium.','A steeper concentration difference, larger exchange area and shorter diffusion distance increase net exchange rate. Water movement through a partially permeable membrane is called osmosis. Diffusion itself does not require cellular energy transfer.','Oxygen concentration is higher outside a respiring cell than inside, so oxygen diffuses in. Carbon dioxide produced by respiration can diffuse out down its own concentration gradient.','Explain why oxygen moves into a respiring cell by diffusion.','Oxygen concentration is higher outside than inside~Particles move randomly~There is net movement down the concentration gradient through the membrane','At equilibrium particles do not stop moving.', 'diffusion')
skill('organisation','Multicellular organisation and skeletons','Cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs work together in organ systems. A skeleton supports the body, protects organs, helps movement and produces blood cells within some bones.','A joint is where bones meet. Cartilage reduces friction at joint surfaces, ligaments connect bone to bone and tendons connect muscle to bone. Hinge joints mainly move in one plane; ball-and-socket joints allow movement in several directions.','In an arm, muscle tissue forms part of the biceps muscle, an organ. The biceps pulls through a tendon on a forearm bone, rotating it about the elbow joint.','Describe the roles of cartilage, ligaments and tendons at a joint.','Cartilage cushions/reduces friction at articulating surfaces~Ligaments connect bones and stabilise the joint~Tendons attach muscle to bone','Bones do not actively contract to cause movement.')
skill('muscles','Antagonistic muscles and movement investigations','Muscles contract to pull and cannot actively push. Antagonistic pairs pull in opposite directions so that a limb can be moved both ways. A force can change movement or deform an object.','During elbow flexion the biceps contracts while the triceps relaxes; for extension their roles reverse. In a model or supervised dissection, identify bone, joint and tendon before relating visible motion to a pull.','Pulling a tendon on a prepared wing bends a joint because the tendon transfers force from muscle to bone. Reversing the motion requires a pull from the opposing muscle system.','Explain why two antagonistic muscles are needed to bend and straighten an elbow.','Muscles pull when contracting and do not actively push~One muscle contracts to bend the elbow~The opposing muscle contracts to straighten it','Relaxing a muscle alone does not actively drive the reverse movement.')
skill('particles','Solids, liquids and gases','The particle model explains physical properties through arrangement, movement and forces. Solid particles vibrate about fixed positions. Liquid particles stay close but move past one another. Gas particles move randomly far apart.','A solid keeps shape and volume; a liquid changes shape but has a fairly fixed volume; a gas spreads to fill its container and is readily compressed. These properties describe a simple model, not the literal colour or size of atoms.','A syringe of gas compresses because particles have large gaps. A syringe of water compresses very little because particles are already close together. The particles themselves do not shrink.','Explain why a gas can be compressed much more than a liquid.','Gas particles have large gaps~Compression reduces the gaps~Liquid particles are close together and cannot be brought much closer','Gases contain particles; the gaps between them are not filled with more gas particles.', 'particles')
skill('state','Changes of state and heating curves','Melting is solid to liquid, freezing is liquid to solid, boiling or evaporation is liquid to gas, and condensation is gas to liquid. These are physical changes: the substance’s chemical identity stays the same.','Heating increases particle energy. During a state change in a pure substance at constant pressure, energy changes particle separation and interactions rather than raising temperature. Evaporation happens at a surface below boiling; boiling occurs throughout a liquid at its boiling point.','Ice at 0°C can absorb energy while melting without its temperature increasing. The resulting liquid contains the same water molecules, now able to move past one another.','Explain the difference between evaporation and boiling.','Evaporation occurs at the surface~It can occur below the boiling point~Boiling occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point for the pressure','Particles do not melt or expand individually when the bulk substance changes state.', 'particles')
skill('gas','Gas pressure and particle motion','Gas pressure results from collisions of moving particles with container walls. Each collision exerts a force; pressure is force per unit area. Increasing temperature increases average particle kinetic energy.','For a fixed gas amount and container volume, heating generally increases pressure because collisions are more frequent and more forceful. Compressing a gas at the same temperature increases collision frequency per unit area.','A sealed rigid container heats up while its gas particle number and volume stay constant. Faster-moving particles transfer more momentum at the walls, so pressure increases.','Explain why squeezing a sealed syringe of air increases its pressure.','Gas volume falls with particle number unchanged~Particles hit the walls more frequently per unit area~Pressure increases','A gas does not need gravity to exert pressure.', 'particles')
skill('atoms','Atoms, elements, compounds and mixtures','An element contains one type of atom. A compound contains atoms of two or more elements chemically bonded in fixed proportions. A mixture contains substances together without those substances being chemically combined.','An atom is the smallest unit retaining an element’s chemical identity; it contains a nucleus and electrons. A molecule is a bonded group of atoms. Elements can have molecules, such as O₂, and not all compounds consist of discrete molecules.','Iron is an element, water is a compound and air is a mixture. A physical separation can separate a suitable mixture, but breaking a compound into elements requires chemical change.','Compare a compound with a mixture using water and salty water as examples.','Water has chemically bonded hydrogen and oxygen in a fixed ratio~Salty water contains dissolved salt and water with variable proportions~A suitable physical method can separate salt from water','A molecule need not contain different elements: oxygen O₂ is an element.')
skill('solution','Solutions and solubility','A solute dissolves in a solvent to form a solution. The solute particles spread throughout the solvent rather than disappearing. Solubility is the maximum amount that dissolves in a given solvent amount at a stated temperature.','A saturated solution cannot dissolve more of that solute under the same conditions. Dissolving rate and solubility are different: stirring can speed up dissolving without increasing the final amount soluble at a fixed temperature.','Crushing sugar increases exposed surface area and speeds dissolving. If a solution is already saturated at that temperature, adding more sugar leaves undissolved solid even after stirring.','Explain why stirring makes sugar dissolve faster but does not necessarily increase its solubility.','Stirring brings fresh solvent into contact with the solute~Dissolving rate increases~The maximum soluble amount at the same temperature is unchanged','Dissolved particles are still present even when the liquid looks clear.')
skill('filter','Filtration and crystallisation','Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a fluid. The residue stays on filter paper and the filtrate passes through. Crystallisation obtains a dissolved solid by concentrating a solution and allowing crystals to form.','Dissolved particles pass through ordinary filter paper with the solvent. For crystallisation gently evaporate some solvent, then cool and allow crystals to form; follow a supervised method for any heating.','Sand and salt can be separated by adding water to dissolve the salt, filtering out sand, then concentrating and cooling the salt solution to obtain crystals.','Describe how to separate a dry mixture of sand and salt.','Add water and stir to dissolve the salt~Filter to retain sand while salt solution passes through~Concentrate the filtrate and crystallise the salt','Filtering salt solution does not remove the dissolved salt.')
skill('distil','Distillation and chromatography','Distillation separates substances using differences in volatility: a liquid evaporates, then its vapour condenses and is collected. Paper chromatography separates dissolved components because they distribute differently between the moving solvent and the paper.','Draw a pencil baseline above solvent level for chromatography, place a small sample spot on it and allow solvent to rise. Different travel distances can suggest different substances, but one spot does not establish purity in all possible solvents.','Distilling salt water collects water while salt remains in the flask. In chromatography an ink splitting into several spots shows it is a mixture of components that separate in that solvent.','Explain how distillation obtains pure water from salt water.','Water evaporates on heating~Vapour cools and condenses in the condenser~Salt is non-volatile under these conditions and remains behind','The condenser turns vapour into liquid; it does not filter dissolved salt.')
skill('energy','Energy stores, transfers and dissipation','Energy is measured in joules and is conserved. Stores include kinetic, thermal, chemical, gravitational and elastic. Transfers occur mechanically, electrically, by heating and by radiation.','Describe where energy starts and ends, and the transfer pathway. Dissipation spreads energy into less useful stores, often the thermal stores of surroundings. The total energy has not disappeared.','A falling ball transfers energy from its gravitational store to its kinetic store. After impact, energy spreads into thermal stores and sound, so the ball’s useful mechanical energy decreases.','Describe the main energy changes when a battery-powered lamp lights a room.','Energy begins in the battery chemical store~Transferred electrically to the lamp~Transferred by light radiation and heating to the surroundings','Energy is transferred or stored; it is not “used up” or created by a device.')
skill('food-energy','Food energy and evaluating heating experiments','Food contains chemical energy. Burning a food sample can transfer energy by heating to water, but a classroom setup captures only part of the food’s energy. Compare energy per gram when masses differ.','Control water volume, starting temperature and apparatus spacing. Measure the mass burned and temperature rise. Energy can escape to air or heat apparatus; incomplete burning also makes a food comparison less reliable.','If a smaller food mass heats the same water through a greater temperature rise, it may supply more captured energy per gram. This conclusion is limited unless energy losses and burning completeness are comparable.','Give two limitations of comparing foods by burning them beneath a tube of water.','Energy escapes to surroundings rather than all heating the water~Incomplete combustion or unequal burning changes captured energy~Control water mass and compare energy per gram','A temperature rise alone cannot compare energy content when sample masses differ.')
skill('resources','Renewable and non-renewable energy resources','Renewable resources are replenished on a human timescale, including sunlight, wind and flowing water. Fossil fuels and nuclear fuels are non-renewable. Each resource has benefits and limitations involving reliability, cost and environmental effects.','Separate an energy resource from an energy store. Compare the service required and the evidence: wind is variable, fossil fuel burning releases carbon dioxide, and nuclear generation produces radioactive waste but low operational carbon emissions.','A town may combine variable solar generation with storage and other generation. A fair evaluation considers daily and seasonal demand rather than assuming one resource always supplies a constant output.','Explain one advantage and one limitation of wind power compared with fossil-fuel electricity generation.','Wind is replenished and needs no fuel burning during operation~It produces low operational greenhouse-gas emissions~Output varies with wind so supply balancing is required','Renewable does not mean impact-free or always available.')
skill('power','Power, electrical energy and cost','Power is the rate of energy transfer: P=E/t. A watt is one joule per second. Electricity bills commonly measure energy in kilowatt-hours; 1 kWh is the energy transferred by 1 kW for 1 hour.','For energy in kWh use power in kW times time in hours, then multiply by a stated price per kWh. Convert watts to kilowatts by dividing by 1000. Reduce use by lowering power, running time or unwanted transfers.','A 1200 W heater runs for 2 hours: 1.2×2=2.4 kWh. At an assumed 25 p/kWh, cost is 60 p. This is an example tariff, not a current price quote.','A 500 W device runs for 3 hours. At a stated 20 p/kWh, calculate its energy use and cost.','500 W=0.5 kW~Energy 0.5×3=1.5 kWh~Cost 1.5×20=30 p','kW is power; kWh is energy.')
skill('efficiency','Efficiency and Sankey diagrams','Efficiency is useful output energy divided by total input energy, or useful output power divided by total input power. Multiply the fraction by 100 for a percentage. A Sankey diagram uses arrow width to represent transferred energy.','Decide what output is useful for the device’s purpose. Input equals total output when all pathways are included. Improve efficiency by reducing unwanted transfers, such as friction, without claiming energy has been destroyed.','A lamp transfers 100 J electrically, producing 25 J as useful visible light and 75 J into unwanted thermal pathways. Efficiency=25/100=0.25=25%.','A motor receives 600 J and provides 450 J of useful mechanical output. Find efficiency and unwanted energy transfer.','Efficiency 450/600=0.75~75%~Unwanted transfer 600−450=150 J','A device cannot have efficiency above 100% when the full energy input is counted.')
skill('variation','Species, inherited and environmental variation','Members of a species share many features and can generally interbreed to produce fertile offspring. Variation consists of differences between individuals; causes include inherited genetic differences, environmental effects and their interaction.','Continuous variation has a range of measurable values, such as height. Discontinuous variation has distinct categories, such as ABO blood group. Eye colour is influenced by several genes and should not be taught as a single simple dominant/recessive trait.','Arm span is usually measured on a continuous scale. A blood group falls into categories. Height can reflect genes and environment, including nutrition, rather than a single cause.','Compare continuous and discontinuous variation, giving one suitable example of each.','Continuous values span a range, e.g. height or arm span~Discontinuous variation has distinct categories, e.g. ABO blood group~Explain why the chosen examples fit','A feature can be influenced by both genes and environment.')
skill('adaptation','Adaptations and changing environments','An adaptation is an inherited feature that can improve survival and reproduction in a particular environment. Structural, behavioural and physiological adaptations help organisms meet challenges such as water loss, temperature or obtaining food.','Explain the link from feature to function to advantage. Individuals can respond to change, but populations evolve through inherited variation across generations. A changed environment may make a previous adaptation less useful.','A cactus has a waxy surface that reduces water loss, helping it survive dry conditions. Thick fur traps insulating air in cold environments but can be disadvantageous in heat.','Explain how a thick fur coat benefits a mammal in a cold environment and why the benefit depends on conditions.','Fur traps air~Air reduces heat transfer from the body~In warmer conditions the same insulation can hinder cooling','Organisms do not choose inherited adaptations because they need them.')
skill('puberty','Adolescence and reproductive systems','Puberty involves hormone-controlled physical changes that lead to sexual maturity; adolescence includes wider physical, emotional and social development. Timing varies between individuals. The reproductive systems produce gametes and support reproduction.','Testes produce sperm; sperm ducts carry sperm, and the penis delivers semen. Ovaries produce egg cells, oviducts carry eggs, the uterus can support pregnancy and the vagina connects to the outside. Use accurate biological vocabulary.','A sperm has a flagellum for movement and genetic material in its nucleus. An egg is much larger and contains cytoplasm supporting early development. Both are gametes, carrying one set of chromosomes.','State the roles of the testes, ovaries and uterus.','Testes produce sperm~Ovaries produce egg cells and reproductive hormones~Uterus supports embryo/fetus development during pregnancy','Puberty does not begin at exactly the same age for everyone.')
skill('cycle','The menstrual cycle','The menstrual cycle includes changes in the ovaries and uterine lining. Menstruation sheds the lining; the lining then rebuilds. Ovulation releases an egg from an ovary. Cycle length and ovulation timing vary.','A 28-day diagram is a teaching model, with ovulation often shown near day 14; this is not a reliable prediction for every person. If implantation occurs, hormonal signals help maintain the uterine lining.','In the model cycle, the lining becomes suitable for implantation after menstruation. If no pregnancy begins, hormone changes eventually lead to its shedding and the start of another cycle.','Describe menstruation and ovulation, and state one limitation of a fixed 28-day cycle diagram.','Menstruation sheds the uterine lining~Ovulation releases an egg~Real cycle lengths and ovulation dates vary','Ovulation is not menstruation, and an egg is not released from the uterus.')
skill('pregnancy','Fertilisation, implantation and fetal development','Fertilisation is fusion of sperm and egg nuclei, usually in an oviduct. The resulting cell divides into an embryo. Implantation is attachment and embedding in the uterine lining, a later event.','The placenta provides an exchange surface between maternal and fetal blood supplies, which normally remain separate. Oxygen and nutrients pass to the fetus; carbon dioxide and other wastes pass back. The umbilical cord links fetus and placenta.','Smoking in pregnancy can reduce oxygen supply to the fetus because carbon monoxide affects haemoglobin, and other smoke substances can cause harm. Evidence-based explanation is different from blaming an individual.','Distinguish fertilisation from implantation and give one role of the placenta.','Fertilisation fuses gamete nuclei, usually in the oviduct~Implantation embeds the embryo in the uterine lining~Placenta exchanges oxygen/nutrients and wastes between separate blood supplies','Maternal and fetal blood do not normally mix directly in the placenta.')
skill('flower','Flower structures and pollination','The stamen consists of an anther that produces pollen and a filament supporting it. The carpel includes stigma, style and ovary containing ovules. Pollination transfers pollen from anther to stigma of a compatible flower.','Insect-pollinated flowers often have petals, scent and nectar to attract insects and sticky pollen. Wind-pollinated flowers often expose anthers and feathery stigmas and make plentiful light pollen. Link each feature to pollen transfer.','An insect brushes against an anther, picks up pollen and deposits some on another flower’s stigma. This transfers male gametes within pollen but is not yet fertilisation.','Compare one adaptation of wind-pollinated flowers with one of insect-pollinated flowers.','Wind example such as exposed feathery stigma~Explain capture of airborne pollen~Insect example such as nectar/petals attracts a carrier','Pollination is transfer of pollen; fertilisation is fusion of nuclei.')
skill('seed','Plant fertilisation, seeds and dispersal','After compatible pollen reaches a stigma, a pollen tube grows towards an ovule. A male nucleus fuses with an egg nucleus in fertilisation. The fertilised ovule develops into a seed, and the ovary commonly develops into a fruit.','Seed dispersal reduces competition with the parent and other seedlings. Wind, animals and explosive release move seeds in different ways. Fruit structures such as wings, hooks or fleshy tissues can support dispersal.','A dandelion seed’s parachute increases air resistance and helps wind transport. A hooked fruit attaches to fur and can be carried away before falling off. Neither process is fertilisation.','Describe the sequence from pollination to seed formation.','Pollen arrives at a compatible stigma~A pollen tube grows to an ovule and male/egg nuclei fuse~The ovule develops into a seed','Plants form seeds after fertilisation; implantation is a term used for embryos in animal reproduction.')
skill('germination','Germination and experimental controls','Germination is the start of growth of a seed into a seedling. Most seeds need water, oxygen and a suitable temperature. Many do not require light for germination, though the growing seedling later needs light for photosynthesis.','Water activates processes and swells tissues; oxygen supports aerobic respiration; temperature affects enzyme activity. Compare conditions while keeping seed type, age and number similar. Record the proportion germinated over a fixed time.','To test the need for water, compare similar seed groups at the same temperature and oxygen availability, changing only water access. Using equal numbers allows meaningful percentage comparisons.','Explain why a seed needs oxygen and water to germinate.','Water activates metabolic processes and allows tissues to swell~Oxygen is needed for aerobic respiration~Respiration supplies energy for growth before sufficient photosynthesis','A seed uses stored reserves initially; it does not obtain its food from soil.')
skill('reaction','Chemical reactions, observations and equations','A chemical reaction forms new substances as atoms rearrange. Possible observations include gas production, a precipitate, temperature change or colour change, but an observation alone may also have a physical explanation.','Word equations put reactants on the left and products on the right. Atoms are conserved; they are not created or destroyed by ordinary chemical reactions. Physical changes such as melting do not change chemical identity.','Magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide. Magnesium oxide has different properties from the starting metal and oxygen. Melting ice instead leaves water molecules chemically unchanged.','Distinguish a chemical reaction from a physical change using one example of each.','Chemical reaction forms new substances, e.g. magnesium oxide from magnesium and oxygen~Physical change retains chemical identity, e.g. melting ice~Explain the contrast using the examples','Bubbles alone do not prove a reaction: boiling also makes bubbles.')
skill('acid','Acids, alkalis, pH and concentration','Acids have pH below 7 in typical aqueous school examples; neutral water has pH 7 near room temperature; alkalis have pH above 7. An alkali is a soluble base. Indicators change colour over pH ranges.','Universal indicator estimates pH; a calibrated pH probe gives a numerical reading. Concentration describes how much solute is present per solution volume. Strength concerns how fully an acid ionises and is different from concentration.','Diluting a given acidic solution usually moves its pH towards 7, though not by a simple linear amount. A concentrated weak acid and a dilute strong acid cannot be compared from those labels alone.','Explain the difference between a concentrated acid and a strong acid.','Concentration is amount of acid per solution volume~Strength concerns extent of ionisation in water~An acid can be strong and dilute, or weak and concentrated','Strong does not mean concentrated, and weak does not mean harmless.')
skill('neutral','Neutralisation and naming salts','An acid reacts with a base in neutralisation. Acid + metal oxide or alkali → salt + water. An acid reacting with a carbonate also produces carbon dioxide. Salt names depend on both reactants.','Hydrochloric acid forms chlorides, sulfuric acid sulfates and nitric acid nitrates. The metal or ammonium part supplies the first part of the name. To make a soluble salt from an insoluble base, add excess base, filter, then crystallise.','Copper oxide + sulfuric acid → copper sulfate + water. Excess copper oxide can be filtered off; water is then partly evaporated so copper sulfate crystals can form on cooling.','Name the salt made from magnesium oxide and hydrochloric acid, and state the other product.','Magnesium supplies the first part~Hydrochloric acid forms chloride~Magnesium chloride and water','Neutralisation need not mean mixing equal volumes: concentrations and reacting amounts matter.')
skill('metals','Metal properties, oxidation and reactivity','Metals typically conduct electricity and heat and can be shaped; non-metals have varied properties and important exceptions, such as conducting graphite. Metal + oxygen commonly forms a metal oxide.','A reactivity series compares tendencies to react. A more reactive metal can displace a less reactive metal from a suitable compound in solution. Evaluate actual observations and conditions rather than assuming every metal reacts visibly with water.','Iron + copper sulfate → iron sulfate + copper. Iron is more reactive than copper, so copper is deposited. Copper cannot displace iron from iron sulfate solution.','Predict whether zinc displaces copper from copper sulfate and explain why, given zinc is more reactive.','Zinc displaces copper~Zinc is more reactive~Products are zinc sulfate and copper','Not every metal is magnetic and not every non-metal is an electrical insulator.')
skill('metal-acid','Metals with acids and water','Many reactive metals react with dilute acids to make a salt and hydrogen. With hydrochloric acid the salt is a chloride. Some very reactive metals react with cold water to form a hydroxide and hydrogen; others require steam or do not react appreciably.','Use the reactivity series and the stated conditions. A small hydrogen sample gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint in a supervised test. Keep flames away from uncontrolled gas collection.','Magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen. Copper does not react appreciably with dilute hydrochloric acid because it is below hydrogen in the reactivity series.','State the products of magnesium with dilute sulfuric acid and describe the hydrogen test.','Magnesium sulfate~Hydrogen~A small sample gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint under supervision','Do not generalise the acid reaction to every metal.')
skill('charge','Charge, static electricity and uses','Electric charge can be positive or negative. Electrons carry negative charge; rubbing suitable insulating materials can transfer electrons. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Charge is conserved in the transfer.','An object gaining electrons becomes negative; one losing them becomes positive. Charged objects can attract neutral materials through charge separation. Static charge is used in applications such as paint spraying but may also cause unwanted sparks.','If a plastic rod gains electrons from a cloth, the rod becomes negatively charged and the cloth positively charged. The rubbing has transferred charge rather than created extra electrons.','Explain why two rods that both gained electrons repel.','Both have gained negative charge~They have the same sign of charge~Like charges repel','Protons do not usually move between objects during ordinary charging by friction.')
skill('current','Circuit diagrams, current and charge conservation','A current is a rate of flow of charge, measured in amperes with an ammeter connected in series. A complete conducting loop and a source of potential difference are needed for a steady current in a simple circuit.','Use standard circuit symbols. In a series circuit current is the same throughout. At a junction total current entering equals total leaving, so currents in parallel branches add to the supply current.','If a parallel circuit has branch currents 0.2 A and 0.3 A, the supply current is 0.5 A. Current is not used up as charge passes through a lamp.','Two parallel branches carry 0.4 A and 0.6 A. Find the supply current and explain the rule used.','Supply current 1.0 A~Branch currents add at a junction~Charge is conserved','Charge flows through the circuit; energy is transferred to components.', 'circuit')
skill('voltage','Potential difference, resistance and circuit models','Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge and is measured in volts. Connect a voltmeter in parallel across a component. Resistance relates potential difference and current through R=V/I.','In a series circuit potential differences across components sum to the supply. Parallel branches across the same two points have the same potential difference. A circuit model can illustrate flow and energy but must not imply that charge is consumed.','Across a 6 Ω resistor with current 0.5 A, V=IR=3 V. Adding a series resistor reduces current at fixed supply voltage; adding a parallel path reduces total resistance.','A resistor has 12 V across it and current 2 A. Calculate its resistance and state how the voltmeter is connected.','R=V/I~12/2=6 Ω~Voltmeter in parallel','V=IR is a relationship; constant resistance requires suitable physical conditions.', 'circuit')

UNITS=[(7,'Working scientifically','safety variables planning burner data evaluate'),(7,'Cells','microscope cell specialised diffusion'),(7,'Movement','organisation muscles planning'),(7,'Particle model','particles state diffusion gas atoms'),(7,'Separating mixtures','atoms solution filter distil'),(7,'Energy costs','energy food-energy resources power'),(7,'Energy transfers','energy efficiency'),(7,'Variation and adaptation','variation adaptation data'),(7,'Human reproduction','puberty cycle pregnancy'),(7,'Plant reproduction','flower seed germination'),(7,'Acids and alkalis','reaction acid neutral'),(7,'Metals and non-metals','metals metal-acid'),(7,'Current and charge','charge current'),(7,'Potential difference and resistance','voltage current')]

skill('foodweb','Food chains, webs and interdependence','A food chain shows feeding relationships and energy transfer. Arrows point from food to consumer. Producers such as plants build organic substances through photosynthesis; consumers obtain them by eating other organisms.','A food web connects chains. A population change can affect several others through food availability, predation and competition. Predictions depend on other links: real ecosystems are more complex than a single chain.','Grass → rabbit → fox means energy and material pass from grass to rabbits to foxes. Fewer rabbits may reduce fox food, while grass may be eaten less, if other factors remain similar.','In algae → water flea → small fish, predict one possible effect of fewer water fleas on each of the other populations.','Small fish may have less food and decline~Algae may face less grazing and increase~Predictions depend on other food-web links and conditions','Food-chain arrows show transfer from food to feeder, not the direction of hunting.')
skill('ecosystem','Ecosystems, competition and sampling','An ecosystem includes a community of organisms interacting with its non-living environment. Competition occurs when organisms require the same limited resource, such as light, water, space or prey.','Random quadrat placement reduces selection bias when estimating plant abundance. Keep quadrat size and counting rules constant and sample enough sites. A transect investigates changes along an environmental gradient rather than random distribution.','If five 1 m² quadrats contain 4,6,5,3,7 daisies, mean density is 25/5=5 daisies/m². An estimate for a comparable 100 m² area is 500 daisies, with uncertainty from sampling.','Explain how to estimate daisy abundance in a field using quadrats.','Place quadrats at randomly selected coordinates~Count consistently and calculate mean per unit area~Multiply by total area and state sampling limitations','Choose sites randomly, not just where plants are easy to see.')
skill('speed','Speed calculations and practical measurements','Average speed is distance/time. Use metres and seconds for m/s, or consistent alternatives. A speed investigation measures a known distance and the time taken to cover it.','Repeat trials, use clear start and finish markers and minimise timing error with suitable equipment. To compare methods control the route and release conditions. Averages must be based on the quantities appropriate to the question.','A trolley covers 1.8 m in 3 s, giving 0.6 m/s. A 0.2 s timing uncertainty matters less for a longer timed interval, if the motion remains comparable.','A trolley travels 2.4 m in 4 s. Calculate its average speed and suggest a timing improvement.','Speed=2.4/4=0.6 m/s~Use a longer known interval or electronic timing~Link the improvement to reduced reaction-time effect','Average speed does not necessarily equal the speed at every point.')
skill('dt','Distance–time graphs','A distance–time graph links distance and elapsed time. For a straight segment its gradient gives speed. A horizontal segment means stationary and a steeper rising segment indicates a greater speed.','Use change in distance divided by change in time between two points on the segment. Do not use a single point’s coordinates unless the segment starts at the origin. Label both axes and units when constructing a graph.','A segment from (2 s,3 m) to (8 s,15 m) has speed (15−3)/(8−2)=2 m/s. The graph is not a picture of a hill or road.','A straight segment runs from (5 s,10 m) to (15 s,40 m). Calculate speed.','Distance change 30 m~Time change 10 s~Speed 3 m/s','An upward line means increasing distance, not necessarily increasing speed.')
skill('force','Forces, resultants and motion','A force is a push or pull measured in newtons. Contact forces include friction and normal contact force; gravity and magnetic forces act without direct contact. A resultant is the overall vector sum.','Balanced forces give zero resultant: an object stays at rest or continues at constant velocity. An unbalanced resultant changes velocity, which may mean changing speed, direction or both. Draw force arrows from the correct object.','For a trolley pushed right with 9 N and resisted left with 4 N, resultant is 5 N right. Equal 9 N opposing forces would not imply the trolley must be stationary; it could move steadily.','A boat has 120 N thrust forwards and 80 N drag backwards. Find the resultant and describe the resulting change in motion.','40 N forwards~Non-zero resultant force~The boat accelerates forwards','A moving object can have balanced forces; motion alone does not require a resultant.')
skill('gravity','Gravity, mass, weight and skydiving','Mass measures the amount of matter in kilograms. Weight is a gravitational force in newtons: W=mg. Gravitational field strength g is force per unit mass, about 9.8 N/kg near Earth’s surface, often rounded to 10 in examples.','During a fall weight acts down and air resistance up. At first the downward resultant increases downward speed. As drag rises, the resultant decreases; at terminal velocity forces balance and speed is constant.','With g=10 N/kg, a 60 kg person weighs 600 N. Opening a parachute sharply increases drag, causing an upward resultant while the person is still moving down, so downward speed decreases.','Explain why a falling skydiver can reach a constant speed.','Weight acts down~Air resistance increases as speed increases~At terminal speed drag equals weight and the resultant is zero','Constant speed does not mean there are no forces.')
skill('crater','Crater investigations and evaluating results','A dropped object transfers gravitational energy into impact, deformation, sound and heating. A crater investigation can test how drop height affects crater diameter while controlling object and surface properties.','Use the same ball mass and size, a level reset surface and a controlled release. Measure crater diameter in more than one direction and repeat each height. A model does not reproduce all meteorite speeds, surfaces or atmospheric effects.','At larger drop heights the same ball generally reaches a greater impact speed and can create a larger crater. An uneven flour surface could change measurements independently of height.','Identify variables and one limitation of a classroom crater experiment.','Change drop height and measure crater diameter~Control ball and surface preparation~A classroom drop has different speeds/materials from a real impact','A trend from a model is evidence under its assumptions, not proof of every real impact outcome.')
skill('elements','Chemical symbols, compound names and formulae','Each element has a chemical symbol with an uppercase first letter and an optional lowercase second letter. A formula gives element types and atom ratios, while a mixture can have variable proportions.','Co is cobalt; CO contains carbon and oxygen. In H₂O, two hydrogen atoms are represented for each oxygen atom. Compounds often have properties very different from their elements. A subscript belongs to the preceding element or bracketed group.','Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine a toxic gas; their compound sodium chloride has very different properties. The compound is not simply a mixture retaining both starting substances’ properties.','Explain what CO₂ represents and how it differs from a mixture of carbon and oxygen.','Carbon and oxygen are chemically bonded~One carbon to two oxygen atoms in each molecule~A mixture is not chemically bonded in a fixed proportion','Capitalisation changes the meaning of a chemical symbol.')
skill('polymer','Polymers and materials','Polymers contain very large molecules built from repeating smaller units. Their properties depend on structure and interactions, so different polymers can be flexible, rigid, insulating or resistant to chemicals. Natural polymers include cellulose and starch.','Choose a material by linking a property to its use and considering its whole life cycle. A polymer is not automatically biodegradable. Reuse and recycling depend on polymer type, contamination and practical collection systems.','An insulating plastic casing reduces contact with conducting wires. A flexible polymer may suit a bag, while a rigid one suits a container; neither use alone proves easy recyclability.','Give one useful polymer property for electrical cable coating and one environmental issue to consider.','Electrical insulation~Reduces contact with conducting wire~Some polymers persist as waste and need appropriate recovery/disposal','Plastic is not one substance with one universal set of properties.')
skill('periodic','The periodic table and element patterns','The periodic table arranges elements by increasing atomic number. Vertical groups contain elements with related chemical behaviour; horizontal rows are periods. Metals are mainly on the left and centre, with non-metals mostly to the right.','Use symbols and table position to identify elements and patterns. Group properties are trends with exceptions, not identical properties in every member. In the school group numbering used here, halogens are Group 7 and noble gases Group 0 (modern Groups 17 and 18).','Sodium and potassium are both Group 1 metals and react with water, but potassium generally reacts more vigorously. Helium and neon are noble gases with very low reactivity under ordinary conditions.','Describe what a group is and why grouping elements is useful.','A vertical column in the periodic table~Members show related chemical properties~This allows informed predictions about reactions','Elements in a group have similarities, not identical properties.')
skill('groups','Groups 1, 7 and 0','Group 1 alkali metals are soft and react with water to produce a hydroxide and hydrogen, becoming generally more reactive down the group. Group 7 halogens are non-metals whose reactivity generally decreases down the group. Group 0 noble gases are very unreactive.','A more reactive halogen can displace a less reactive halogen from a halide solution. Noble gases have stable outer electron arrangements. Only trained staff carry out hazardous alkali-metal or halogen demonstrations.','Chlorine displaces bromine from bromide solution, but bromine does not displace chlorine from chloride solution. Neon is used in suitable lighting applications because it is unreactive and emits characteristic light in a discharge.','Given chlorine is more reactive than iodine, predict chlorine’s reaction with potassium iodide solution.','Chlorine displaces iodine~Products include potassium chloride and iodine~A more reactive halogen replaces a less reactive one','Halogen reactivity trends in the opposite direction to Group 1 reactivity.')
skill('transition','Transition metals and useful properties','The central transition metals commonly have high densities and melting points, conduct heat and electricity, form coloured compounds and can act as catalysts. These are general tendencies with exceptions.','Link properties to applications and distinguish elements from their compounds. A catalyst speeds a reaction and is regenerated overall. Some transition-metal compounds are hazardous even when a familiar bulk metal has common everyday uses.','Copper conducts electricity well, so it is used in wires. Iron is used in structural alloys, while iron can catalyse industrial reactions. A coloured copper compound is chemically different from copper metal.','Explain two uses of transition metals using their properties.','Electrical conductor such as copper used in wiring~Strength/high melting point supports an appropriate structural use~Catalytic activity supports a suitable chemical use','A transition metal and its compounds need not have the same colour or hazards.')
skill('lungs','Gas exchange and ventilation','Air enters the trachea, then bronchi and bronchioles, reaching alveoli. Alveoli provide a large exchange area, thin walls and a rich blood supply. Oxygen diffuses into blood and carbon dioxide diffuses out.','During inhalation the diaphragm contracts and flattens, while rib movements increase chest volume; pressure in the lungs falls and air moves in. During quiet exhalation muscles relax and elastic recoil reduces volume. Ventilation moves air; respiration is a cellular process.','Thin alveolar walls shorten the diffusion distance. Blood flow and ventilation maintain concentration differences, allowing continuing gas exchange rather than reaching static equilibrium.','Explain how the diaphragm helps inhalation.','It contracts and flattens~Thoracic volume increases and lung pressure falls~Air moves in down a pressure difference','Lungs do not actively suck oxygen into cells; pressure differences move air.')
skill('drugs','Drugs, alcohol, smoking and vaping','A drug changes how the body works; some are medicines used under appropriate guidance and others can cause harm or dependence. Alcohol affects nervous-system function. Tobacco smoke contains harmful substances including tar and carbon monoxide.','Nicotine is addictive. Smoke damage can impair gas exchange and cilia function. Vaping is not risk-free and is not suitable for children or non-smokers; it should not be described as harmless simply because tobacco is not burned. Evaluate evidence and avoid advertising claims.','A damaged gas-exchange surface reduces effective oxygen uptake. Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin and reduces oxygen transport, giving a mechanism linking smoke exposure to reduced exercise capacity.','Explain two ways cigarette smoke can reduce effective oxygen supply.','Damage to alveoli reduces effective exchange area~Carbon monoxide reduces haemoglobin oxygen carriage~Connect either mechanism to reduced oxygen reaching tissues','“Less harmful than smoking” would not mean safe or appropriate for a young non-smoker.')
skill('nutrition','Nutrients, balanced diets and deficiency','Carbohydrates and fats supply energy; proteins support growth and repair. Vitamins and minerals are needed in small amounts. Fibre supports digestive function and water is required for transport and many cellular processes. A balanced diet meets individual needs over time.','Compare evidence using portions and energy needs rather than labelling one food as always good or bad. An energy imbalance can affect body mass, and deficiency can arise when a particular nutrient is insufficient. Health is influenced by many interacting factors.','Iron is needed to make haemoglobin; inadequate iron can contribute to anaemia. Vitamin C deficiency can cause scurvy. Fibre is not digested into glucose by human digestive enzymes but remains useful in the diet.','State the roles of protein, fibre and iron.','Protein supports growth and tissue repair~Fibre helps digestive function~Iron is required for haemoglobin','Vitamins and minerals do not provide significant dietary energy.')
skill('food-tests','Food tests and interpreting evidence','Food tests identify particular substances rather than measuring total nutritional quality. Iodine changes from orange-brown to blue-black with starch. Benedict’s reagent forms a coloured precipitate on suitable heating with reducing sugars. Biuret reagent turns purple with protein.','The emulsion test produces a cloudy white emulsion when lipids are present. Use separate samples and controls to avoid contamination and follow supervised methods; ethanol is flammable and must be kept away from naked flames.','A blue-black iodine result supports starch being present. A negative starch test does not mean the sample has no carbohydrate, because simple sugars require a different test.','Describe a positive starch test and explain why a negative result does not show that all carbohydrate is absent.','Add iodine solution~A blue-black change indicates starch~Other carbohydrates such as sugars may be present despite a negative starch result','A food test result identifies the tested substance, not the overall health value of a food.')
skill('digestion','Digestion, absorption and enzymes','Digestion breaks large insoluble food molecules into smaller soluble ones. The mouth starts mechanical and chemical processing; the stomach mixes food, and much digestion and absorption occur in the small intestine. The large intestine absorbs water.','Enzymes are biological catalysts with specific substrates. Amylase digests starch into sugars, proteases digest proteins and lipases digest fats. Bile helps emulsify fats and neutralise acidic stomach contents; it is not an enzyme. Villi provide a large absorption area.','A large protein cannot be absorbed directly like an amino acid. Proteases break it down, then smaller products cross the intestinal wall into circulation. Gut microorganisms also contribute to normal digestive processes.','Explain how enzymes and villi help supply digested nutrients to the blood.','Enzymes break large molecules into smaller soluble products~Villi increase surface area~Thin exchange surfaces and blood supply support absorption','Digestion and respiration are different processes.')
skill('sound','Wave properties and sound transmission','A wave transfers energy without an overall transfer of the medium. Sound is a longitudinal wave: particles oscillate parallel to the direction of energy transfer, forming compressions and rarefactions. Sound needs a medium and cannot cross a vacuum.','Amplitude relates to sound intensity and perceived loudness; frequency relates to pitch. Frequency is oscillations per second in hertz. Wavelength is distance between equivalent points. Wave speed=f×λ.','Increasing a speaker’s oscillation frequency raises pitch. Increasing amplitude makes the sound more intense without necessarily changing its pitch. A sound pulse travels through air while each air particle oscillates locally.','Explain the difference between increasing a sound’s amplitude and increasing its frequency.','Amplitude increase increases intensity/loudness~Frequency increase raises pitch~They describe different wave properties','Air particles do not travel from the speaker all the way to the listener.', 'wave')
skill('ear','Hearing, echoes and sound speed','The outer ear channels sound to the eardrum, which vibrates. The ossicles transmit vibrations to the cochlea, where receptors produce signals sent through the auditory nerve. Excessive sound exposure can damage hearing.','An echo is reflected sound. For a return trip, distance to a reflector equals speed×echo time/2. Sound travels at different speeds in different media and commonly faster in solids than gases. Loudness and speed are different properties.','If an echo returns after 0.4 s and sound speed is 340 m/s, total travel distance is 136 m and the reflector is 68 m away. The division by 2 accounts for the journey there and back.','A sound echo returns after 0.2 s. Using 340 m/s, find the distance to the wall.','Total distance 340×0.2=68 m~Sound travels to the wall and back~Wall distance 34 m','Making a sound louder does not make it travel faster through the same air.')
skill('light','Light, reflection and refraction','Light is an electromagnetic wave and can travel through a vacuum. In ray diagrams straight lines show direction in a uniform medium. At a reflecting surface, angle of incidence equals angle of reflection, measured from the normal.','Refraction changes light direction when its speed changes at a boundary, except when it travels along the normal. Entering glass from air typically bends a ray towards the normal; leaving bends it away. Draw normals perpendicular to the surface.','A ray incident at 35° to the normal reflects at 35° to the normal, or 55° to the surface. In glass the ray travels more slowly than in air.','A ray strikes a plane mirror at 40° to the normal. Give the reflection angle and its angle to the mirror surface.','Reflection angle 40° to normal~Surface is 90° to normal~Angle to surface 50°','Angles in reflection and refraction rules are measured from the normal.', 'rays')
skill('colour','Colour, lenses and the electromagnetic spectrum','White light contains a range of visible wavelengths. A filter transmits some wavelengths and absorbs others. Objects appear coloured because of the light they reflect into the eye. A convex lens can bring parallel rays towards a focus.','The electromagnetic spectrum runs radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma in order of increasing frequency. All travel at the same speed in a vacuum; their interactions and uses differ. Lens diagrams simplify real behaviour.','A red object under blue light may look dark because little blue light is reflected. A red filter does not add red light: it transmits available red wavelengths and absorbs much of the rest.','Explain why a red object can look dark under blue light.','It mainly reflects red wavelengths~Blue illumination contains little/no red~Much of the incident blue light is absorbed rather than reflected','A filter does not create the colour it transmits.', 'rays')
skill('combustion','Combustion, decomposition and physical changes','Combustion is reaction with oxygen that transfers energy to the surroundings. Hydrocarbon fuels undergoing complete combustion form carbon dioxide and water; incomplete combustion can form carbon monoxide or soot. Thermal decomposition breaks a compound down using heating.','Describe reactants and products with word equations. A temperature or state change need not be chemical. Conservation of atoms helps explain why a compound can change into simpler substances without matter disappearing.','Calcium carbonate → calcium oxide + carbon dioxide is a thermal decomposition. Melting wax is physical, while burning its vapour is chemical. Those processes can occur near each other in a candle.','Classify melting ice and heating calcium carbonate to form calcium oxide and carbon dioxide, explaining each.','Melting ice is physical~Water remains chemically water~Calcium carbonate decomposition is chemical because new substances form','Heating does not automatically mean burning.')
skill('mass','Conservation of mass and gas exchange','In an ordinary chemical reaction atoms rearrange but their total number and total mass are conserved. In a closed system total reactant mass equals total product mass. In an open container mass can enter or leave as gas.','Define the system before comparing masses. A decrease can mean gas escaped, while an increase can mean oxygen from the air joined the material. Use a suitable balance and safe closed apparatus where the method requires it.','An open flask containing reacting acid and carbonate may lose mass as carbon dioxide escapes. The gas still exists outside the flask, so the total mass of all substances is conserved.','Explain why magnesium gains mass when burned in air.','Magnesium reacts with oxygen~Oxygen atoms become part of magnesium oxide~Including the oxygen input, total mass is conserved','Mass is not created when a solid product is heavier than the starting metal.')
skill('chemical-energy','Bonds, exothermic and endothermic reactions','Breaking chemical bonds requires energy; forming bonds transfers energy out. An exothermic reaction transfers energy to surroundings overall, while an endothermic reaction transfers energy from surroundings overall.','A measured temperature rise suggests an exothermic reaction if other influences are controlled. A fall suggests endothermic behaviour. Insulation and a lid can reduce unwanted transfers; compare amounts and starting conditions fairly.','A hand-warmer reaction transfers energy to its surroundings, increasing their temperature. In an endothermic cold pack, the reacting system gains energy from the surroundings, which cool.','Explain why an exothermic reaction can make its surroundings warmer, referring to bonds.','Energy is needed to break reactant bonds~More energy is released when product bonds form~The net transfer warms the surroundings','Bond breaking takes energy; it does not release energy by itself.')
skill('catalyst','Catalysts and enzyme activity','A catalyst increases reaction rate without being used up overall. It provides a reaction pathway with lower activation energy. Enzymes are biological catalysts and depend on suitable conditions.','Compare reaction rate using the same amounts, concentration, temperature and apparatus. Collect gas volume against time or use another suitable measurable change. A catalyst changes how quickly equilibrium is reached, not the overall energy change of the reaction.','For decomposition of hydrogen peroxide, a suitable catalyst gives faster oxygen production. More gas at an early time means a higher rate, not necessarily more final gas from the same limiting reactant amount.','Explain why faster gas production does not necessarily mean more gas will be produced in total.','Catalyst increases rate~Same reactant amount can produce the same final gas amount~The reaction reaches completion sooner','A catalyst is regenerated overall, although it participates in intermediate steps.')
skill('respiration','Aerobic respiration and exercise','Aerobic respiration transfers energy from glucose in cells using oxygen. The word equation is glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water. Energy supports processes including muscle contraction, temperature regulation and growth.','Respiration takes place continually in plant and animal cells. During exercise muscle energy demand rises, so breathing and heart rate generally increase to support gas exchange and transport. Breathing is movement of air, not the chemical reaction of respiration.','A runner’s muscles require greater rates of energy transfer. Increased ventilation brings more oxygen to alveoli and increased blood flow delivers it to tissues while carrying carbon dioxide away.','Explain why breathing rate usually increases during exercise.','Muscles require faster energy transfer~Aerobic respiration needs oxygen and produces carbon dioxide~Increased ventilation supports oxygen supply and carbon dioxide removal','Respiration is not the same as breathing.')
skill('anaerobic','Anaerobic respiration, fermentation and biotechnology','When oxygen supply cannot meet demand, human muscles can transfer energy anaerobically from glucose, producing lactate. Yeast anaerobic respiration forms ethanol and carbon dioxide; this is fermentation. Anaerobic pathways transfer less energy per glucose than aerobic respiration.','Yeast fermentation is used in bread making and producing alcoholic drinks. Investigations can compare gas production per minute while controlling yeast quantity, glucose concentration, temperature and time. Biological activity has an optimum range and can decline at high temperatures.','Carbon dioxide from yeast makes dough rise; baking changes the structure and kills the yeast. Raising temperature speeds enzyme activity only up to a suitable range, not indefinitely.','State the products of yeast anaerobic respiration and explain how one helps bread making.','Ethanol~Carbon dioxide~Carbon dioxide gas makes dough rise','Human muscle anaerobic respiration and yeast fermentation do not have identical products.')
skill('photosynthesis','Photosynthesis and uses of glucose','Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical stores in glucose. Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen. Chlorophyll absorbs light in chloroplasts. Plants also respire at all times, including in daylight.','Glucose can be used in respiration, converted to starch for storage, used to make cellulose or combined with mineral nutrients to build other molecules. Much of a plant’s dry mass comes from carbon dioxide incorporated from the air.','A green leaf in light can make glucose and release oxygen. At night photosynthesis stops without light, but respiration continues, using oxygen and producing carbon dioxide.','State the photosynthesis word equation and two possible uses of the glucose made.','Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, with light~Respiration or starch storage~Cellulose production or making other suitable compounds','Plants do not take their food from soil; they make organic substances using photosynthesis.', 'photosynthesis')
skill('leaf','Leaf structure, minerals and limiting factors','A leaf’s broad surface intercepts light. Thin tissues shorten diffusion distances; palisade cells often contain many chloroplasts. Stomata allow gas exchange, and vascular tissues transport water and sugars.','Light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature can limit photosynthesis. Nitrate ions are needed for amino acids and proteins; magnesium is needed for chlorophyll. Increasing one factor helps only if it is limiting.','Adding more light may initially increase oxygen production. If carbon dioxide becomes limiting, further light produces little increase. A magnesium deficiency can reduce chlorophyll and cause yellowing.','Explain why increasing light intensity may eventually stop increasing photosynthesis rate.','Light initially limits rate~Another factor such as carbon dioxide becomes limiting~Increasing light alone cannot remove that new limit','Mineral nutrients are not a plant’s energy source.', 'photosynthesis')
skill('plant-practical','Pondweed, starch tests and evaluating photosynthesis evidence','Oxygen production from pondweed can indicate photosynthesis rate, but net gas output also reflects respiration. Gas volume is often more reliable than bubble count because bubbles vary in size. A starch test indicates stored carbohydrate.','Control temperature and carbon dioxide while changing light. Allow time to acclimatise. In a supervised starch test, soften the leaf, decolourise it with ethanol in a hot-water bath away from flames, rinse and add iodine. A blue-black result indicates starch.','A destarched plant partly covered with opaque material and exposed to light can test light’s role. Only suitable uncovered green regions should accumulate starch, provided other conditions allow photosynthesis.','Give two limitations of counting pondweed bubbles and one way to improve the evidence.','Bubbles have variable volumes~Some oxygen dissolves or is used in respiration~Collect gas volume over a fixed time and repeat while controlling conditions','Bubble counts are a proxy, not a direct count of glucose molecules made.')
skill('investigate-candle','Investigation: candle burning and air volume','A candle flame requires fuel, oxygen and sufficient energy to maintain combustion. Covering it with a container changes its access to fresh air. A simple relationship between container volume and burning time is a model with limitations.','Compare container volumes while controlling candle, wick length, initial conditions and placement. Time a clearly defined endpoint, repeat and graph volume against mean burning time. Hot glass and flames need a supervised risk assessment.','Larger air volumes may support longer burning, but the flame can extinguish before every oxygen molecule is used. Changes in gas composition and heat losses also influence the outcome.','Identify variables for testing how container volume affects candle burning time.','Independent: container air volume~Dependent: time until extinction~Control candle/wick, initial conditions and timing rule','Do not conclude that an extinguished candle proves all oxygen is gone.')
skill('investigate-bounce','Investigation: bouncing balls','A falling ball transfers gravitational energy to kinetic energy. Impact can store energy elastically and dissipate some into thermal stores and sound. Rebound height depends on the ball, surface and conditions.','Change ball type while controlling release height, surface and release technique, then measure the maximum rebound height. Repeat trials and compare means and spread. Video against a scale can reduce reading difficulty.','For a fixed ball mass, a rebound to 60% of drop height corresponds approximately to retaining 60% of its gravitational energy at the top, assuming the same reference level and negligible size effects.','Explain why rebound height is usually lower than drop height and how to make a fair comparison of two balls.','Some energy is dissipated to surroundings~Keep release height and surface constant~Repeat and measure rebound height consistently','Energy is conserved overall even though useful mechanical energy decreases.')
skill('investigate-bungee','Investigation: bungee extension and safe clearance','A hanging mass has weight and stretches an elastic cord. Extension is stretched length minus original length. Elastic behaviour depends on the material and need not remain proportional over all loads.','Change mass systematically and measure extension after settling. Control cord length and material, use modest supervised loads and provide clearance. Plot force against extension, using W=mg where appropriate, and do not extrapolate an unsafe maximum.','A cord originally 20 cm long reaching 27 cm has extension 7 cm. Another load producing length 30 cm gives extension 10 cm, not 30 cm.','State how to measure extension and give two controls in a bungee investigation.','Subtract original length from stretched length~Use the same cord/material/original length~Keep measurement position and settling/timing procedure consistent','Elastic cords do not necessarily follow a straight-line force–extension relationship.')
skill('investigate-conduction','Investigation: conduction through metals','Thermal conduction transfers energy through particle interactions, with mobile electrons contributing in metals. Comparing conductors requires controlling their dimensions and heating conditions.','Use rods of matching length and cross-section with the same heat source. A supervised indicator such as wax melting can show energy transfer, but endpoint judgement and contact differences create uncertainty. Repeat rather than relying on one result.','If a wax marker melts earlier on one otherwise comparable rod, that rod may transfer thermal energy faster. A thicker rod or stronger heating could also explain a shorter time, so control them.','Explain two controls needed to compare thermal conduction in metal rods.','Same length and cross-sectional area~Same heating arrangement/starting temperature~Link controls to avoiding alternative explanations','Faster heating in one setup does not prove material type was the only cause.')
skill('investigate-cupcake','Investigation: paper cases and falling motion','Nested paper cupcake cases can model how weight and drag affect a fall. More cases increase mass with a similar shape, although geometry and airflow may also change. Terminal speed occurs when drag balances weight.','Drop from a fixed safe height, release without pushing and measure time using video or repeated timings. Control case type and orientation. State whether the height is sufficient to approach terminal speed; average fall speed alone does not establish it.','Two nested cases have greater weight than one. They may need a greater falling speed to produce matching drag, but the investigation must check repeatability and shape consistency.','Explain why measuring average falling speed does not prove a paper case reached terminal velocity.','Average combines changing speeds~Terminal velocity requires constant velocity with balanced forces~Use position–time/video evidence of steady speed','The folder name “cupcakes” refers here to falling paper cases, not a baking reaction.')
skill('investigate-salt','Investigation: salt amount and dissolving time','Dissolving rate depends on conditions including temperature, agitation, particle size and concentration. Solubility limits the amount that can dissolve at a stated temperature. Adding more solute is not the same experiment as changing solute type.','Change salt mass while controlling water volume, temperature, stirring and grain size. Define the endpoint consistently and note any mass that does not fully dissolve. Do not record “longer time” indefinitely for a saturated solution.','Increasing mass may lengthen the time to dissolve fully, while at sufficiently high mass some salt remains. That is evidence of saturation under those conditions, rather than a failed stopwatch.','Explain why a dissolving-time investigation becomes difficult once the solution is saturated.','Maximum soluble amount has been reached at those conditions~Undissolved salt remains~A finite time to complete dissolving is no longer a suitable endpoint','Solubility and dissolving rate are different quantities.')
skill('investigate-diffusion','Investigation: temperature and diffusion','Particles move randomly and their average kinetic energy increases with temperature. A colour-spreading experiment can model diffusion, but bulk water movement can also transport colour.','Change temperature while controlling liquid volume, dye amount, vessel dimensions and starting position. Avoid stirring and let water settle. Record a defined colour boundary or time to an agreed endpoint, repeat and evaluate convection as a confounding factor.','Dye may spread faster in warm water, but uneven heating can create convection currents. A cautious conclusion distinguishes the observed pattern from proof that diffusion alone caused all visible movement.','Suggest two controls and one limitation when comparing colour spreading in warm and cool water.','Same vessel/water volume/dye amount~Avoid stirring and use the same release method~Convection or subjective boundary judgement can affect results','Visible spreading can involve convection as well as molecular diffusion.')

