skill('magnet','Permanent magnets, fields and the Earth','A permanent magnet has north- and south-seeking poles. Like poles repel; unlike poles attract. A magnetic field is a region where a magnetic material or magnet experiences a force. Iron, nickel, cobalt and some alloys are magnetic.','Outside a bar magnet field lines run north to south; closer spacing indicates a stronger field. A compass’s north-seeking end points roughly towards geographic north because the Earth’s field there behaves broadly like a magnetic south pole.','A compass aligns with the local field. An unmagnetised iron object can be attracted near either pole because it becomes induced magnetically; attraction alone does not prove an object is a permanent magnet.','Explain why attraction alone cannot show that a metal object is a permanent magnet.','A magnetic material can be induced by a nearby magnet~It can then be attracted~Repulsion provides stronger evidence that the tested object is itself a magnet','The north-seeking end of a compass is its north pole, not its south pole.')
skill('electromagnet','Electromagnets, relays and fair comparisons','A current creates a magnetic field. A coil concentrates the field, and an iron core can increase it. An electromagnet can be switched and its strength changed by changing current, turns or core conditions.','Compare one factor at a time; increasing turns may also change resistance and current, so monitor current if it must be controlled. Avoid overheating coils. Relays use an electromagnet to operate a switch; scrapyard magnets release loads when current is removed.','In a relay a small control current magnetises a coil, attracts an armature and closes another circuit. The mechanical switching separates the control action from the load circuit.','Describe two ways to increase an electromagnet’s strength and one control for a fair test of coil turns.','Increase current~Add turns or an appropriate iron core~For a turns comparison keep core/geometry consistent and monitor/control current','A current-carrying coil produces a field even without an iron core.')
skill('work','Work done and simple machines','Work is energy transferred by a force moving an object through a distance in the force’s direction: W=Fs. Work is measured in joules. A machine can reduce required force by increasing the distance through which that force acts.','Identify the relevant distance and direction, convert units and multiply. In an ideal machine, input work equals useful output work; real machines dissipate some energy. A lever or pulley does not create energy.','A 40 N force acting over 3 m does 120 J of work. An ideal pulley that halves lifting force requires twice the pulling distance for the same lifted height.','Calculate work done by a 25 N force moving an object 4 m in the force’s direction, and explain why a machine cannot create extra energy.','W=Fs~25×4=100 J~Energy is conserved; reduced force trades against increased input distance in an ideal machine','A large force with no movement does no mechanical work on that stationary object.')
skill('moments','Levers, turning effects and moments','A moment is a force’s turning effect around a pivot. Moment=force×perpendicular distance from pivot to the line of action, measured in N m. A lever can increase a turning effect by increasing this distance.','For rotational equilibrium total clockwise moment equals total anticlockwise moment. Use perpendicular distances, not an arbitrary length to the force application point. Balance of moments alone does not guarantee zero resultant force.','A 20 N force at 0.3 m gives moment 6 N m. To balance it with an opposite force at 0.6 m requires 10 N, because 10×0.6=6.','A 12 N force acts 0.5 m from a pivot. What opposing force at 0.3 m balances the moment?','Moment 12×0.5=6 N m~Opposing force satisfies F×0.3=6~F=20 N','The distance is perpendicular to the force’s line of action.', 'lever')
skill('thermal','Temperature, energy and conduction','Temperature relates to average particle kinetic energy; an object’s thermal store also depends on how much material it contains and what it is. Energy transfers spontaneously from hotter to cooler regions.','Conduction transfers energy through particle interactions without bulk material flow. In metals mobile electrons also help transfer energy. A large cooler bath may contain more thermal energy than a small hotter cup, depending on the reference comparison.','A metal spoon handle warms when its other end is in hot water because energy spreads through the metal. The metal atoms do not travel from the water to the handle.','Explain why temperature alone cannot compare the thermal stores of a cup of water and a bath.','Temperature relates to average particle energy~The bath contains much more material~Total energy also depends on mass and material','Heat is an energy transfer, not a substance stored in objects.')
skill('convection','Convection, radiation and insulation','Convection involves bulk movement in fluids: warmer, less dense regions can rise while cooler denser regions sink in a gravitational field. Thermal radiation is electromagnetic transfer and can cross a vacuum.','Insulation reduces transfer pathways. Trapped air reduces conduction and limits convection; shiny low-emissivity surfaces reduce thermal radiation. A vacuum reduces conduction and convection but does not prevent radiation.','A vacuum flask uses a vacuum gap to reduce conduction and convection, reflective surfaces to reduce radiation and an insulated stopper to limit other transfers. No real flask completely prevents energy transfer.','Explain how trapped air and a shiny surface can help insulation.','Air is a poor conductor~Small pockets limit convection~Shiny surfaces have low emissivity and reduce thermal radiation','Warm fluid rises due to density differences; “heat rises” is not a complete explanation.')
skill('atmosphere','The atmosphere and carbon cycle','Dry air near Earth’s surface is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and about 1% other gases. Water vapour varies. Carbon moves between atmosphere, organisms, oceans and rocks through several processes.','Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide, while respiration, combustion and decomposition return carbon to the environment. Long-term burial stores carbon; burning fossil fuels transfers stored carbon into the atmosphere on much shorter timescales.','A growing tree takes in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis and incorporates carbon into organic material. Cutting and burning the tree returns much of that carbon, so a full carbon accounting must consider both uptake and release.','Name two processes that add carbon dioxide to air and one that removes it.','Respiration~Combustion or decomposition~Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide','The carbon cycle tracks carbon atoms, not a one-way journey of energy.')
skill('climate','Greenhouse effect, climate evidence and human activity','Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared radiation, influencing the balance between incoming solar energy and outgoing radiation. The natural greenhouse effect keeps Earth warmer than it would otherwise be. Human activities increase some greenhouse-gas concentrations.','Burning fossil fuels, land-use change and some agricultural processes contribute. Distinguish weather over short periods from climate patterns over decades. Evaluate long-term data and multiple evidence sources, not one cold day or one local record.','Increasing atmospheric carbon dioxide changes the outgoing energy balance and contributes to warming. Consequences can include sea-level rise, altered weather patterns and ecosystem changes; local effects vary.','Explain how increased carbon dioxide can contribute to global warming.','Carbon dioxide absorbs outgoing infrared radiation~It re-emits radiation and changes net energy loss~A higher equilibrium temperature is reached under the changed energy balance','The greenhouse effect is not primarily a hole in the ozone layer.')
skill('extraction','Metal extraction, displacement and resources','An ore contains enough of a useful substance for extraction to be worthwhile under the conditions. A metal’s reactivity influences extraction: carbon can reduce oxides of some metals below carbon in the reactivity series; more reactive metals commonly require electrolysis.','Separate a general rule from specific conditions and economics. Displacement provides evidence of relative reactivity. Finite deposits, energy needs, pollution and transport all affect the costs of using metals.','Iron oxide can be reduced using carbon-based processes because iron is below carbon in the series. Aluminium is more reactive and industrial extraction uses electrolysis of suitable molten compounds.','Explain why carbon reduction works for some metal oxides but not for aluminium oxide under ordinary extraction conditions.','Carbon can displace/reduce metals less reactive than itself~Aluminium is above carbon~Aluminium extraction therefore requires a different process such as electrolysis','Being common in rocks does not mean an element is easy to extract.')
skill('recycle','Recycling and life-cycle evaluation','Reusing keeps a product in service; recycling processes materials into new products. Both can conserve resources, but impacts depend on collection, sorting, energy use, transport and product quality.','Compare a whole life cycle: extraction, manufacture, use and disposal. Use numerical data if supplied and state trade-offs. A claim of “recyclable” does not mean that every local system can or does recycle the item.','Recycling aluminium usually avoids much of the energy-intensive ore extraction stage. Contamination and mixed materials can add sorting costs, so design for separation may improve practical recovery.','Give two benefits and one limitation of metal recycling.','Conserves finite ore/resources~Can reduce extraction energy and associated impacts~Collection, contamination or processing requires resources','Recycling is beneficial in many cases but not a process with zero energy use.')
skill('selection','Evolution by natural selection','Individuals in a population vary, and some variation is inherited. When a trait improves survival and reproduction in a particular environment, the alleles associated with it can become more frequent over generations.','Describe variation first, then the environmental selection pressure, differential reproduction and inheritance. Mutations do not arise because an organism needs a particular change. Individuals do not deliberately evolve; population characteristics change over generations.','If a dry habitat favours a heritable water-saving feature, individuals carrying it may leave more offspring. Over many generations that feature can become more common without any conscious choice.','Explain natural selection when an inherited trait improves survival in a changed environment.','Inherited variation already exists~Individuals with an advantageous trait tend to survive/reproduce more~Offspring inherit associated alleles, changing their frequency over generations','Survival alone is not enough; reproduction and inheritance link the generations.')
skill('evidence-evolution','Darwin, competing ideas and evidence','Darwin and Wallace developed explanations of evolution through natural selection. Scientific ideas are assessed using evidence and can be refined. Fossils, similarities between organisms and genetic evidence support change and common ancestry.','Distinguish a well-supported scientific theory from an untested guess. Acquired changes such as a trained muscle are not automatically inherited. Modern genetics explains inheritance in ways that were unavailable to early evolution researchers.','A giraffe does not genetically lengthen its offspring’s neck simply by stretching. In a natural-selection model, inherited differences affect reproductive success, and population frequencies change over generations.','Explain why stretching a muscle during life is not the same process as inherited evolution.','It is an acquired individual change~It need not alter inherited genetic information~Evolution involves inherited population changes across generations','A scientific theory is an explanatory framework supported and tested by evidence.')
skill('biodiversity','Extinction, endangered species and biodiversity','Extinction occurs when a species has no living individuals left. Risk can increase through habitat loss, environmental change, disease, invasive species or overexploitation. Biodiversity includes the variety of species and genetic diversity within them.','Conservation can protect habitats, reduce pressures and maintain breeding populations. Captive breeding or seed banks can help but do not automatically restore a functioning ecosystem. Evaluate evidence and the reason a particular intervention addresses the threat.','Protecting a breeding habitat can support food, shelter and reproduction at once. A breeding programme without suitable habitat for release may preserve individuals but not a self-sustaining wild population.','Explain one habitat-based conservation method and one limitation of captive breeding alone.','Protect/restore habitat to support survival and reproduction~Captive breeding may maintain numbers/genetic stock~Without suitable wild habitat, release may not sustain a population','Endangered means at risk of extinction, not already extinct.')
skill('inheritance','DNA, genes, chromosomes and inherited variation','DNA carries genetic information. A gene is a section of DNA that contributes to a functional product; different versions are alleles. Chromosomes are long DNA molecules associated with proteins. Most human body cells have chromosomes in pairs.','Gametes carry one chromosome from each pair, and fertilisation restores pairs. Many traits depend on several genes and the environment. A mutation is a change in DNA and may be harmful, neutral or beneficial depending on context.','A child inherits genetic material from both biological parents, but is not an exact average of their visible characteristics. New combinations of alleles contribute to variation.','Explain the relationship between DNA, genes and chromosomes.','Chromosomes contain long DNA molecules~Genes are sections of DNA~Alleles are alternative versions of a gene','Dominant does not mean more common, stronger or better.')
skill('dna-practical','DNA evidence, structure and extraction','DNA has a double-helix structure. Models of its structure were developed using multiple lines of evidence and contributions from several researchers, including Franklin, Wilkins, Watson and Crick. A classroom extraction makes DNA-rich material visible but does not reveal its molecular structure.','In a supervised fruit extraction, detergent disrupts membranes, salt can help DNA aggregate and cold ethanol helps precipitate DNA. Keep ethanol away from flames and do not taste materials. The visible strands contain many molecules and may include impurities.','A white precipitate in an extraction is consistent with DNA-rich material from many cells. Seeing it does not show the sequence of bases, prove sample purity or reveal individual genes by eye.','Explain the roles of detergent and ethanol in a classroom DNA extraction.','Detergent disrupts lipid membranes~DNA is released from cells/nuclei~Ethanol reduces DNA solubility so DNA-rich material precipitates','Visible strands are not a magnified view of one double helix.')
skill('cross','Genetic crosses and probability','For a simple single-gene model, a dominant allele is expressed with one copy, while a recessive phenotype requires two recessive copies. Genotype names the allele combination; phenotype is the expressed characteristic.','Write parental genotypes and possible gametes, then combine them in a Punnett square. State the model assumptions. Each offspring is a new probabilistic event: a 1-in-4 chance does not require exactly one in every four siblings.','For Aa×Aa, combinations are AA, Aa, Aa, aa. Genotype ratio is 1:2:1; with complete dominance the phenotype ratio is 3:1. Use a hypothetical trait rather than claiming that all real human traits fit it.','In a simple dominant/recessive model, cross Aa with aa and find the chance of the recessive phenotype.','Gametes A or a from Aa and a from aa~Offspring Aa or aa in equal probability~Recessive phenotype probability 1/2 or 50%','A Punnett square predicts probabilities, not a guaranteed family sequence.', 'genetics')
skill('gm','Genetic modification: benefits and limitations','Genetic modification changes an organism’s genetic material using biotechnology. It can introduce or alter a trait, but the effect depends on the gene, organism and environment. Selective breeding instead chooses parents with desired inherited traits over generations.','Evaluate a specific use with evidence. Possible benefits include producing medicines or reducing crop damage; concerns can involve ecological effects, access, resistance and unintended consequences. Do not assume all applications have identical risks or benefits.','Bacteria can be engineered to produce human insulin under controlled industrial conditions. A crop altered for pest resistance poses a different evaluation involving farming systems and effects on other organisms.','Distinguish genetic modification from selective breeding and give a potential benefit of one named use.','GM directly changes genetic material using biotechnology~Selective breeding chooses parents over generations~A specific justified benefit, such as producing insulin','A judgement should concern a particular application, rather than treating every modified organism as identical.')
skill('waves','Transverse and longitudinal waves','A transverse wave oscillates perpendicular to energy transfer; a longitudinal wave oscillates parallel. Amplitude is maximum displacement, wavelength is spacing between corresponding points and frequency counts complete oscillations each second.','Wave speed=f×λ and period=1/f. Distinguish a moving wave pattern from the local oscillation of material. Electromagnetic waves do not need a material medium, whereas sound does.','A wave of frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 0.8 m has speed 4 m/s and period 0.2 s. A larger amplitude does not necessarily change the speed in the same linear medium.','A wave has frequency 4 Hz and wavelength 1.5 m. Calculate speed and period.','v=fλ=6 m/s~T=1/f~0.25 s','Wavelength is not the distance a particle moves during one oscillation.', 'wave')
skill('ultrasound','Ultrasound and echo applications','Ultrasound is sound at frequencies above about 20 kHz, beyond the typical upper limit of young human hearing. It remains a mechanical wave and needs a medium. Reflections occur at boundaries where acoustic properties change.','Time-of-flight measurements can estimate distances using speed×time/2 for a reflected pulse. Medical imaging uses returning signals to build a picture; gel improves coupling by reducing air gaps. Cleaning uses physical effects in a liquid.','A pulse returning after 0.00004 s in tissue at 1500 m/s travels 0.06 m in total, so the reflecting boundary is 0.03 m deep. This is a simplified travel-time model.','Explain why a reflected ultrasound distance calculation divides speed×time by two.','Recorded time includes outward and return journeys~Speed×time gives total path length~Depth is half for a direct reflected path','Ultrasound is not an electromagnetic or ionising wave.')
skill('em','Electromagnetic waves, uses and ionising radiation','The electromagnetic spectrum is radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray and gamma in increasing frequency. Wavelength decreases in that direction. All electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum.','Uses depend on interactions: radio for communication, infrared for thermal imaging and X-rays for imaging through soft tissue. Higher-energy ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays can ionise matter and damage cells. Risk depends on exposure and the radiation involved.','X-rays are useful because different tissues absorb them differently. Exposure is controlled to obtain useful information while reducing unnecessary dose. Infrared mainly transfers energy through heating rather than ionisation.','Explain why X-rays can be useful for imaging but require exposure control.','Different materials/tissues absorb X-rays differently~A detector records the contrast~Ionising radiation can damage cells so exposure is managed','All EM waves have the same vacuum speed; higher frequency does not mean faster.')
skill('earth','Earth’s layers and rock types','Earth has a thin solid crust, a mantle, a liquid outer core and solid inner core. The mantle is mainly solid rock that deforms slowly over long timescales, rather than a global ocean of liquid magma.','Igneous rocks form when molten material solidifies. Sedimentary rocks form from deposited material that becomes rock, often through compaction and cementation. Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks change under heat and pressure without melting.','A slowly cooled magma can form large crystals because they have longer to grow. Rapidly cooled lava usually has smaller crystals. Melting a metamorphic rock and cooling it makes an igneous rock.','Compare how igneous and metamorphic rocks form.','Igneous: molten material cools and solidifies~Metamorphic: existing rock changes with heat/pressure~Metamorphism does not require melting','The mantle is largely solid and the outer core is liquid.')
skill('sediment','Sedimentary rocks, fossils and weathering','Weathering breaks rock down in place through physical, chemical or biological processes. Erosion transports material. Sediments can be deposited, compacted and cemented into sedimentary rock. Fossils preserve evidence of former organisms.','Freeze–thaw can widen cracks when water freezes and expands. Chemical weathering changes minerals. Fossils commonly form where rapid burial and suitable conditions limit destruction; their record is incomplete and biased.','A shell buried in fine sediment can leave a fossil as sediment becomes rock. High temperatures during melting or strong metamorphism can destroy evidence, so fossils are usually associated with sedimentary rocks.','Distinguish weathering from erosion and explain why burial can help fossil formation.','Weathering breaks down rock in place~Erosion moves material~Burial can reduce scavenging/decay and protect remains during preservation','The fossil record is incomplete; absence of a fossil does not prove an organism never existed.')
skill('rock-cycle','The rock cycle and timescales','The rock cycle connects rock types through weathering, erosion, transport, deposition, burial, heat, pressure, melting and cooling. It has multiple pathways and no single required starting point.','Name the actual process on each arrow. Sedimentary rock can metamorphose without melting; any suitable rock can melt and later cool into igneous rock. Uplift and exposure allow renewed weathering over geological timescales.','An igneous rock exposed at the surface may weather into sediment, become sedimentary rock after burial and cementation, then become metamorphic under increased heat and pressure. Melting is a further distinct step.','Describe a route from sedimentary rock to igneous rock, including the processes.','Sedimentary rock melts under suitable conditions~Molten material is magma/lava~Cooling and solidification form igneous rock','Heat and pressure without melting form metamorphic rock, not automatically igneous rock.')
skill('space','Night sky, Solar System and scale','A star produces radiation through processes including nuclear fusion; a planet or moon is seen mainly by reflected starlight. Our Solar System contains the Sun, planets, moons and smaller bodies. It is part of the Milky Way galaxy.','Distances are huge: an astronomical unit is approximately Earth’s mean Sun distance, and a light-year is a distance light travels in one year. Scale models must preserve ratios; showing both body sizes and orbit distances to one scale is often impractical.','Neptune is far beyond Earth’s orbit, while the nearest stars are vastly farther away again. A diagram with large visible planets and closely spaced orbits usually uses different scales for size and distance.','Explain what a light-year measures and give one limitation of a typical Solar System classroom diagram.','Light-year measures distance~Distance travelled by light in one year~Planet sizes and orbit distances are often not shown to a single common scale','A light-year is not a unit of time.')
skill('orbits','Orbits, seasons and eclipses','Gravity continually changes an orbiting body’s direction. Earth rotates approximately once each day and orbits the Sun approximately once each year. Its tilted rotational axis explains seasonal changes in day length and sunlight angle.','Seasons are not mainly caused by changing Sun distance. A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon lies between Sun and Earth and its shadow reaches Earth; a lunar eclipse occurs when Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon. Orbital tilt means eclipses do not happen every month.','When the Northern Hemisphere tilts towards the Sun it has longer days and more direct sunlight; the Southern Hemisphere has the opposite season. A solar eclipse requires a particular alignment at new moon.','Explain why the hemispheres have opposite seasons.','Earth’s axis is tilted~One hemisphere tilts towards the Sun while the other tilts away~Sunlight angle and day length differ, affecting energy received','Earth’s rotation causes day and night; its tilt and orbit cause seasons.', 'orbit')
skill('moon','Moon phases and space exploration','The Moon reflects sunlight. About half its surface is sunlit at a time, and phases arise because we see different fractions of that illuminated half as it orbits Earth. Ordinary phases are not caused by Earth’s shadow.','New moon has the illuminated side mostly facing away from Earth; full moon has it mostly facing Earth. Exploration uses observations, satellites, probes and sometimes people; evaluate scientific value, cost, risk and limitations rather than assuming every mission has the same purpose.','A first-quarter Moon appears half illuminated from Earth even though half the whole spherical Moon is sunlit. The name refers to its approximate position through the phase cycle.','Distinguish a normal Moon phase from a lunar eclipse.','Phases arise from changing views of the sunlit half~Earth’s shadow is not responsible for normal phases~A lunar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes through Earth’s shadow','The Moon does not produce its own visible light.', 'orbit')
skill('friction','Friction, drag and deformation','Friction opposes relative sliding or its tendency at a contact. Drag opposes motion through a fluid and generally increases with speed. Forces can change motion or shape, producing stretching, compression or bending.','Use force diagrams and specify the object. Streamlining can reduce drag for suitable conditions. An elastic deformation is reversed when forces are removed; a plastic deformation is permanent. The details depend on material and load.','A bicycle needs a driving force to balance drag and rolling resistance at a steady speed. With no driving force those resistive forces give a backward resultant and reduce forward speed.','Explain why a cyclist needs a driving force while moving at constant speed on a level road.','Drag and friction act backwards~Driving force balances them~Zero resultant maintains constant velocity','Constant speed needs balanced forces, not necessarily no forces.')
skill('spring','Stretching springs and Hooke’s law','Extension is the increase in length. For a spring below its limit of proportionality, force is proportional to extension: F=kx. The spring constant k measures stiffness. Extension in metres gives k in N/m.','Measure original length, then add known loads and record settled lengths. Subtract original length and plot force against extension. Repeat, check for permanent stretching and use a safe load limit. Proportionality need not continue indefinitely.','A spring extends 0.04 m under 6 N, giving k=6/0.04=150 N/m. If it stays in the proportional region, 3 N gives 0.02 m extension.','A spring with k=200 N/m extends by 0.03 m. Calculate force and state a condition for using Hooke’s law.','F=kx~200×0.03=6 N~Spring must be within its proportional region','Use extension, not total length, and convert centimetres to metres when needed.', 'spring')
skill('pressure','Pressure, force and area','Pressure is force per unit area: p=F/A. One pascal is one newton per square metre. For the same perpendicular force, spreading it over a larger area reduces pressure.','Use consistent area units. Convert square units carefully: 1 cm²=0.0001 m². Rearrange F=pA or A=F/p. A pressure increase can result from greater force, smaller area or both.','A 600 N person supported over 0.03 m² exerts 20,000 Pa. Snowshoes increase contact area, reducing pressure and helping avoid sinking into snow.','A 120 N force acts over 0.04 m². Calculate pressure and explain what doubling the area would do at the same force.','p=120/0.04=3000 Pa~Pressure is inversely proportional to area for fixed force~Doubling area halves pressure to 1500 Pa','Do not treat an area conversion as a length conversion.')
skill('liquid-pressure','Liquid pressure, hydraulics and gas investigations','A stationary liquid exerts pressure in all directions, increasing with depth because of the weight of liquid above. In a connected enclosed fluid, an applied pressure change can transmit through the fluid, forming the basis of hydraulics.','Different piston areas give different forces at the same pressure: F=pA. A larger output force trades against smaller output movement in an ideal hydraulic system. Gas experiments must also distinguish changes in temperature, volume and particle amount.','If pressure is 2000 Pa, a 0.01 m² piston feels 20 N and a 0.05 m² piston 100 N. This does not create energy: the larger piston moves a shorter distance for the same transferred fluid volume.','Explain why a hole lower down a water-filled container can produce a stronger jet.','Greater depth has more liquid above~Liquid pressure increases with depth~The larger pressure difference drives a faster outflow','Pressure and force are different; the same pressure can act over different areas.')

